A new method for global illumination just dropped: “Gaussian Light Transport” skips neural networks entirely and solves the full rendering equation as a 13D Gaussian mixture over space, direction, normals, and materials.

The result? 10–23× faster training (7–12 min on RTX 4080), only 1–6 MB memory, and real-time 1080p rendering at 50–75 fps—while matching or beating neural baselines on visual accuracy and speed. Each pixel needs just 76 Gaussians (vs. tens of thousands for neural methods) thanks to clever culling.

For game engines, AR, or interactive design, this means you can bake physically-correct, view-independent lighting in minutes and ship a tiny asset—no lightmaps, no neural nets, no heavy memory.

